  message: "Phase 1 shipped in PR #3313 (merged 2026-04-23): NameMatcher utility + framework_patterns.py base_class/parent_base_class retrofit. Remaining Level 2 scope downgraded to todo_soft and moved to follow-up: decorator/annotation retrofit via NameMatcher.match(), di_resolution.py interface-name lookup retrofit, io_boundary.py lookup_with_module audit, polyglot coverage fixture, lint rule flagging raw re.match on symbol-meta strings. The first-party payoff site (base_class matching) is live; remaining sites are hardening."  # 5b45
- op: discuss  # dfc8
  at: '2026-04-23T16:12:49Z'  # dfc8
  by: agent  # dfc8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dfc8
  clock: 5  # dfc8
  nonce: dfc8  # dfc8
  message: "Practical-implications assessment of the unimplemented Level 2 scope (2026-04-23):\n\nDECORATOR / ANNOTATION / PARAMETER_TYPE RETROFIT — low practical payoff.\nDecorator YAML patterns are receiver-qualified (^(app|router)\\.(get|post)$) so NameMatcher's terminal-segment fallback rarely helps — stripping the source to 'get' doesn't match a pattern anchored to 'app.get'. The parameter_type case is the one real win ('Depends' vs 'fastapi.Depends') but narrow. These patterns work as intended; deferring doesn't meaningfully expand the bug class we're trying to contain.\n\nDI_RESOLUTION.PY RETROFIT — moderate real-world miss.\nAny Spring / Guice / Dagger / .NET Core DI codebase has bindings like bind(IUserRepository.class).to(UserRepositoryImpl.class). The DI regex extracts the short name; the symbol dict is keyed by fully-qualified name. Silent interface→impl edge loss for whichever direction doesn't match. Users with DI-heavy Java/Kotlin/C# stacks see partial dispatch graphs. Same-shape bug as WI-zigah, different pipeline stage, substantive user population affected.\n\nIO_BOUNDARY.PY LOOKUP_WITH_MODULE AUDIT — unknown-unknowns.\nRound 2 dogfood gave strong evidence the critical path works (all 7 urllib sites matched correctly post-WI-zigah, so lookup_with_module does handle dotted modules). But lookup_with_module's code was never read to verify. Possible hidden edge cases: FFI pseudo-namespace interactions, case-sensitivity, prefix collisions. Likely fine. ~1 hour to close.\n\nLINT / ENFORCEMENT RULE — medium-term regression risk, no current-day impact.\nLevel 2 as shipped is containment-by-convention: future developers have to *remember* to use NameMatcher. They will look at existing code and see re.compile(self.decorator) still using raw regex. In six months someone writes a new linker matcher, uses raw regex, reintroduces the exact bug class. The lint rule is the difference between 'we fixed this' and 'we fixed this durably' — what turns Level 2 from a two-site patch into structural containment.\n\nPOLYGLOT COVERAGE FIXTURE — substantive gap for Level 1b and for language extensions.\nRight now WI-mafik (Level 1b) has no audit instrument — to find the JS/TS / Go / Rust / Java analog bugs, someone has to repeat the WI-zigah dogfood process by hand per language. The fixture would be one file per language where adding a new import/call construct automatically fails CI if the analyzer misses it. Also protects against future analyzer-coverage regressions: Python 3.12 type params, TS 5 import type, new framework dispatch conventions. Without it, Level 1b is 5x more expensive than it needs to be.\n\nPRIORITY STACK for the remaining Level 2 work:\n\n1. Polyglot coverage fixture — highest leverage. Unblocks Level 1b, protects indefinitely against analyzer-coverage regressions. ~2-4 hours for Python + property-test scaffolding; each additional language incremental.\n2. Lint rule — converts Level 2 from convention to enforcement. ~1-2 hours. Cheap insurance against 6-month regression.\n3. di_resolution retrofit — moderate user impact on Java/Kotlin/C# DI stacks. ~1-2 hours.\n4. io_boundary audit — defensive. ~30-60 minutes. Low expected finding.\n5. Decorator/annotation retrofit — low payoff given receiver-qualified pattern structure. Defer indefinitely unless a concrete miss is reported.\n\nThe gap between 'what shipped' (PR #3313) and 'full Level 2' is not that the shipped scope is fragile — it's that the containment is convention-based rather than enforced, and the instrument for auditing Level 1b doesn't yet exist. Those are the two that actually matter. The others are cleanup."  # dfc8
- op: discuss  # 467d
  at: '2026-04-23T16:48:10Z'  # 467d
  by: agent  # 467d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 467d
  clock: 6  # 467d
  nonce: 467d  # 467d
  message: "Phase 2 implementation findings (2026-04-23):\n\nRETROFITTED (shipped): _annotation_re, _param_type_re now use NameMatcher. YAML annotation corpus (aspnet, cli-rust, tauri, rust-web, etc.) and parameter_type corpus (fastapi, jakarta-cdi, stapler) have no dispatch-set triplets, so terminal-segment fallback safely extends coverage to qualified source spellings (e.g., `^Depends$` now matches `fastapi.Depends`, `^RestController$` matches `org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController`).\n\nNOT RETROFITTED (structural reason, regression guard added): _decorator_re stays on raw `re.compile`. The decorator YAML corpus uses DISPATCH-SET TRIPLETS — celery.yaml pairs `^task$` for bare `@task` with `^(celery|app)\\.task$` for `@app.task`; jakarta-cdi.yaml pairs `^ApplicationScoped$` with `^jakarta\\.enterprise\\.context\\.ApplicationScoped$`. Terminal-fallback would break mutual exclusion: `^task$` would also match `app.task` via the terminal, firing both rules and doubling concept enrichment on every celery/CDI codebase. Four regressions (bottle, celery, express, jakarta-cdi) surfaced this. Added test_decorator_bare_pattern_does_not_match_qualified_source as a guard against accidental re-retrofit.\n\nAUDIT RESULTS (not vulnerable, no retrofit needed): \n- di_resolution.py line 641 sym_by_name lookup: binding.interface_name / impl_name are always short names (rsplit at lines 251, 256, 277, 382, 395; _GUICE_IMPLEMENTED_BY strips FQN); sym.name is also short by hypergumbo's symbol model. Both sides canonical. No matcher-boundary asymmetry.\n- io_boundary.py line 833 lookup_with_module: already canonicalizes via _by_qualified index with dot-normalized alias (WI-vipur, line 154-156), and short-name fallback via _by_short[p.name]. No retrofit needed.\n\nSTRUCTURAL INSIGHT FOR WI-pisab scope: the Level 2 invariant (terminal-fallback canonicalization at matcher boundaries) only applies to SEMANTIC-ASSERTION matchers (is-a / has-type / is-annotated-with) where the same entity can legitimately be spelled multiple ways. It does NOT apply to DISPATCH-SET matchers (which-rule-fires) where YAML authors have written mutually-exclusive patterns. This distinction should be explicit in the Level 3 canonical-form IR design (sibling item).\n\nREMAINING LEVEL 2 SCOPE: lint rule (enforce NameMatcher at assertion matchers, forbid raw re.compile), polyglot coverage fixture (cross-language analyzer call-site coverage). PR #<pending> ships the Phase 2 retrofit."  # 467d
